1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,784
This is ECE 320, video 4. The topic is functions and the stack in RISC-V.

2
00:00:07,234 --> 00:00:25,410
After this video, you can trace a call and a return, with the P C and R A. You can save and restore registers on the stack, and you know which side must save each register. And you can solve assignment 2, question 1 parts d and e, and question 2.

3
00:00:25,860 --> 00:00:39,727
One function can be called from many places. The caller and the function use the same 32 registers. So both sides follow fixed rules. Slide 18 lists the six steps of a function call.

4
00:00:40,177 --> 00:00:47,046
Step 1. The caller puts the parameters where the function can access them: in the argument registers.

5
00:00:47,496 --> 00:00:51,080
Step 2. The caller transfers control to the function.

6
00:00:51,530 --> 00:00:58,847
Step 3. The function acquires the local storage that it needs. Step 4. It performs its task.

7
00:00:59,297 --> 00:01:07,127
Step 5. The function puts the result where the caller can get it. It also restores the registers that it used.

8
00:01:07,577 --> 00:01:15,555
Step 6. The function returns control to the point of origin. It must return to the place that called it this time.

9
00:01:16,005 --> 00:01:23,067
Steps 3 and 5 are a pair. Step 3 saves old register values. Step 5 puts them back.

10
00:01:23,517 --> 00:01:39,239
Slide 17 lists the 32 registers. The hardware knows only the numbers x0 to x31. In assembly, you can also use the symbolic names. Each name tells the job of the register in a call.

11
00:01:39,689 --> 00:01:47,881
a0 to a7 are x10 to x17, the eight argument registers. The caller puts the parameters here.

12
00:01:48,331 --> 00:01:54,966
a0 and a1 also hold the return values. The function puts its result in a0.

13
00:01:55,416 --> 00:02:02,371
R A is x1, the return address register. It holds the address where the function must return.

14
00:02:02,821 --> 00:02:06,490
Why registers? Registers are faster than memory.

15
00:02:06,940 --> 00:02:21,724
Slide 20 shows the code in memory. Every instruction is 4 bytes, so the addresses go up by 4. The caller wants sum of a and b. At 1000, move copies a, from s0, into a0.

16
00:02:22,174 --> 00:02:30,899
At 1004, the second move copies b into a1. That is step 1: the parameters are in the argument registers.

17
00:02:31,349 --> 00:02:42,848
At 1008, add immediate puts 1016 into R A. 1016 is the address after the jump. It is the place to return to.

18
00:02:43,298 --> 00:02:49,826
At 1012, the jump goes to sum. The P C becomes 2000. That is step 2.

19
00:02:50,276 --> 00:02:58,212
At 2000, sum adds a0 and a1. The result goes into a0, the return value register.

20
00:02:58,662 --> 00:03:07,259
At 2004, jump register sets the P C to the value in R A. So the P C becomes 1016.

21
00:03:07,709 --> 00:03:14,387
The caller continues at 1016, after the call. It finds the result in a0.

22
00:03:14,837 --> 00:03:27,637
Slide 22 asks: why does sum end with jump register, and not with a plain jump? Look at two places that call sum. The second call site is an example at 3008.

23
00:03:28,087 --> 00:03:43,788
Assume sum ends with jump to 1016. The return to call site 1 is correct. But call site 2 also returns to 1016. That is wrong. A plain jump always goes to one fixed place.

24
00:03:44,238 --> 00:03:58,531
Now sum ends with jump register R A. Each caller puts its own return address in R A: 1016 or 3016. So each call returns to its own next instruction.

25
00:03:58,981 --> 00:04:06,043
Slide 24. Before, the call needed two instructions: add immediate to set R A, then a jump.

26
00:04:06,493 --> 00:04:21,597
RISC-V has one instruction that does both: jump and link, J A L. Jump and link sum is at 1008. The next instruction is now at 1012, so R A becomes 1012.

27
00:04:22,047 --> 00:04:34,633
Jump and link saves P C plus 4 in R A. That is the address of the next instruction, 1012. Then it sets the P C to the address of sum, 2000.

28
00:04:35,083 --> 00:04:42,358
Why? Function calls are very common. So make the common case fast. It also makes the program smaller.

29
00:04:42,808 --> 00:04:51,000
Slide 25 is the summary. Call a function with jump and link and its label. Return with jump register R A.

30
00:04:51,450 --> 00:04:59,941
Slide 26 is the next example. The function Leaf computes g plus h, minus i plus j, and returns it.

31
00:05:00,391 --> 00:05:06,151
The parameters g, h, i and j are in a0, a1, a2 and a3.

32
00:05:06,601 --> 00:05:11,891
f is in s0. Leaf also needs one temporary register, s1.

33
00:05:12,341 --> 00:05:29,472
Here is the problem. The caller may keep its own values in s0 and s1, for example 70 and 80. Leaf writes s0 and s1. So it must save the old values first, and restore them before it returns. Where can it save them?

34
00:05:29,922 --> 00:05:48,823
The ideal place is a stack: a last in, first out queue. Push places data on it. Pop removes data. The stack is in memory. S P, the stack pointer, x2, points to it. The stack grows down, from high to low addresses. Here S P is 6000, an example.

35
00:05:49,273 --> 00:05:58,767
Push, part 1. Add immediate S P, S P, minus 4. S P moves down by one word, to 5996.

36
00:05:59,217 --> 00:06:11,078
Push, part 2. Store word s0 at offset 0 from S P. The old s0, 70, is now in memory at 5996.

37
00:06:11,528 --> 00:06:15,859
Now the code can use s0. Here s0 becomes 11.

38
00:06:16,309 --> 00:06:24,799
Pop, part 1. Load word s0 from offset 0 from S P. s0 gets its old value, 70, back.

39
00:06:25,249 --> 00:06:34,593
Pop, part 2. Add 4 to S P. S P is 6000 again. The old value stays in memory, but that space is free now.

40
00:06:35,043 --> 00:06:51,555
Here is the code for Leaf from slide 28. On the right are the stack and four registers, with example values. g, h, i and j are 5, 6, 1 and 2. The caller keeps 70 in s0 and 80 in s1.

41
00:06:52,005 --> 00:07:01,413
First, make room for 2 items. Add minus 8 to S P. S P goes from 6000 to 5992.

42
00:07:01,863 --> 00:07:10,077
Store word s1 at offset 4. The old s1, 80, is now at 5996.

43
00:07:10,527 --> 00:07:19,124
Store word s0 at offset 0. The old s0, 70, is now at 5992.

44
00:07:19,574 --> 00:07:27,105
Now the work. s0 gets g plus h, which is 11. s1 gets i plus j, which is 3.

45
00:07:27,555 --> 00:07:34,893
Subtract s1 from s0, and put the result in a0. a0 is 8, the return value.

46
00:07:35,343 --> 00:07:45,391
Restore the registers. Load s0 from offset 0, and s1 from offset 4. s0 is 70 and s1 is 80 again.

47
00:07:45,841 --> 00:07:51,644
Add 8 to S P. This deletes the 2 items. S P is 6000 again.

48
00:07:52,094 --> 00:08:04,617
Jump register R A goes back to the caller. S P, s0 and s1 have the same values as before the call. Only a0 changed: it holds the result, 8.

49
00:08:05,067 --> 00:08:12,747
Slide 29 shows the stack in three pictures. Before the call, S P points to the last word of the caller.

50
00:08:13,197 --> 00:08:21,325
During the call, S P is 8 bytes lower. The saved s1 and s0 are between the old and the new S P.

51
00:08:21,775 --> 00:08:33,060
After the call, S P is back at its old value. The saved values are still in memory, but the space is free. A function must leave S P as it found it.

52
00:08:33,510 --> 00:08:49,809
What if a function calls a function? Slide 31 uses sumSquare. It returns mult of x and x, plus y. This first version saves nothing. x is in a0 and y is in a1. The addresses are examples.

53
00:08:50,259 --> 00:08:56,595
The caller calls sumSquare with jump and link at 1000. R A becomes 1004.

54
00:08:57,045 --> 00:09:09,951
sumSquare copies x into a1, so a1 no longer holds y. Then it calls mult at 2004. R A becomes 2008. The value 1004 is lost.

55
00:09:10,401 --> 00:09:17,185
mult computes x times x in a0, and returns to 2008. This return is correct.

56
00:09:17,635 --> 00:09:32,675
sumSquare adds a1, but a1 holds x, not y. Then jump register R A goes to 2008 again, not to 1004. A call inside a function clobbers R A and the argument registers.

57
00:09:33,125 --> 00:09:40,400
The fix: before the call to mult, save the return address and y on the stack. Restore them after the call.

58
00:09:40,850 --> 00:09:49,746
Loads and stores to save registers are expensive. So the RISC-V calling convention divides the registers into two groups.

59
00:09:50,196 --> 00:10:05,961
Group 1 is preserved across a call: S P, g p, t p, and the saved registers s0 to s11. The caller can rely on these values. So if the callee uses one, the callee must save it and restore it.

60
00:10:06,411 --> 00:10:20,406
Group 2 is not preserved: the argument registers a0 to a7, R A, and the temporary registers t0 to t6. If the caller needs one after the call, the caller must save it.

61
00:10:20,856 --> 00:10:28,941
The Saver column on slide 17 says the same thing. Callee for the green registers. Caller for the orange ones.

62
00:10:29,391 --> 00:10:42,085
So the choice of registers matters. If Leaf uses t0 and t1 instead of s0 and s1, it saves nothing. It needs 4 instructions, not 10, and no stack.

63
00:10:42,535 --> 00:10:56,145
The stack also holds local variables that do not fit in registers. Slide 34 shows all the parts that a call can put on the stack. Before the call, S P points to the caller's data.

64
00:10:56,595 --> 00:11:01,929
First, the saved return address. A function that calls another function needs it.

65
00:11:02,379 --> 00:11:07,861
Then the saved argument registers, if the function needs their values after a call.

66
00:11:08,311 --> 00:11:14,519
Then the saved s registers that the function uses. Last, the local variables, if any.

67
00:11:14,969 --> 00:11:27,449
During the call, S P points to the bottom of this area. After the call, S P has its old value again. This part of the stack is the procedure frame, or activation record.

68
00:11:27,899 --> 00:11:43,857
Slides 35 and 36 compile sumSquare with the stack. The example values are x equal to 3 in a0, and y equal to 4 in a1. The caller called from 1000, so R A is 1004.

69
00:11:44,307 --> 00:11:50,557
Push. Add minus 8 to S P. S P goes to 5992.

70
00:11:51,007 --> 00:11:56,255
Store R A at offset 4. The return address 1004 is safe.

71
00:11:56,705 --> 00:12:09,676
Store a1, which is y, at offset 0. sumSquare needs y after the call, and a1 is not preserved. x is not saved, because the code does not need x after the call.

72
00:12:10,126 --> 00:12:16,355
Move x into a1. Now a0 and a1 both hold x, the parameters for mult.

73
00:12:16,805 --> 00:12:26,981
Jump and link mult at 2016. R A becomes 2020. mult returns 9 in a0. It may also change a1.

74
00:12:27,431 --> 00:12:32,701
Restore y. Load word a1 from offset 0. a1 is 4 again.

75
00:12:33,151 --> 00:12:38,335
Add a0 and a1. a0 is 9 plus 4, which is 13.

76
00:12:38,785 --> 00:12:43,542
Load word R A from offset 4. R A is 1004 again.

77
00:12:43,992 --> 00:12:54,360
Pop. Add 8 to S P, so S P is 6000 again. Jump register R A returns to 1004, with 13 in a0.

78
00:12:54,810 --> 00:13:03,258
Now assignment 2, question 1 d. Compile compare and sub. Use the calling convention. Pause the video and try it.

79
00:13:03,708 --> 00:13:16,359
First, find the calls. compare calls sub, so its jump and link changes R A. So compare must save R A on the stack. sub calls nothing. It is a leaf, and it needs no stack.

80
00:13:16,809 --> 00:13:27,817
The solution passes a in a1 and b in a2. The result goes in a0. The label is s u b t, because sub is the name of an instruction.

81
00:13:28,267 --> 00:13:39,509
Here is the solution code, with example values a equal to 5 and b equal to 3. compare is called from 1000, so R A is 1004.

82
00:13:39,959 --> 00:13:47,191
compare makes room for one word, and stores R A there. S P is 5996.

83
00:13:47,641 --> 00:13:56,495
Jump and link subt at 2008. R A becomes 2012. Without the save, 1004 would now be lost.

84
00:13:56,945 --> 00:14:05,265
subt computes a1 minus a2. a0 is 5 minus 3, which is 2. It returns to 2012.

85
00:14:05,715 --> 00:14:10,131
compare sets t1 to 1. This is the value for the if part.

86
00:14:10,581 --> 00:14:19,562
a0 is 2, which is not less than zero. So the branch goes to exit, and skips the instruction that sets t1 to 0.

87
00:14:20,012 --> 00:14:23,212
At exit, a0 gets t1, which is 1.

88
00:14:23,662 --> 00:14:33,390
Load R A back, add 4 to S P, and jump register R A. compare returns to 1004 with a0 equal to 1.

89
00:14:33,840 --> 00:14:47,664
Why are no other registers saved? compare does not use a1 or a2 after the call. If a is less than b, the branch is not taken, t1 becomes 0, and compare returns 0.

90
00:14:48,114 --> 00:14:55,133
Assignment 2, question 1 e. Compile the recursive function fact. Pause the video and try it.

91
00:14:55,583 --> 00:15:10,260
Plan. fact calls itself, so it must save R A. fact also needs n after the call, to compute n times fact of n minus 1. n is in a1, which is not preserved. So fact must save n too.

92
00:15:10,710 --> 00:15:26,283
Here is the chain of calls for fact of 3. Each call passes n minus 1. fact of 0 returns 1. Then each call multiplies: 1 times 1 is 1, 2 times 1 is 2, and 3 times 2 is 6.

93
00:15:26,733 --> 00:15:36,355
Here is the solution code. The trace is fact of 3, called from 1000. So n is 3 in a1, and R A is 1004.

94
00:15:36,805 --> 00:15:51,141
n is not less than 1, so go to recurse. Push a frame of 2 words: R A, 1004, and n, 3. Then a1 becomes 2, and jump and link calls fact. R A becomes 2036.

95
00:15:51,591 --> 00:15:59,249
fact of 2 does the same. It pushes 2036 and 2. a1 becomes 1, and it calls fact again.

96
00:15:59,699 --> 00:16:10,686
fact of 1 pushes 2036 and 1, and calls fact with 0. Now there are three frames, and S P is 5976.

97
00:16:11,136 --> 00:16:20,608
In fact of 0, n is less than 1. a0 becomes 1, and it returns to 2036. This call pushes no frame.

98
00:16:21,058 --> 00:16:31,362
Back in fact of 1. Load n, which is 1, into t0, and load R A. Pop the frame. a0 becomes 1 times 1, which is 1.

99
00:16:31,812 --> 00:16:39,321
Back in fact of 2. t0 is 2. a0 becomes 1 times 2, which is 2. Pop and return.

100
00:16:39,771 --> 00:16:56,241
Back in fact of 3. t0 is 3, and R A is 1004 again. a0 becomes 2 times 3, which is 6. S P is 6000 again. Each call had its own frame. Multiply is from the reference card.

101
00:16:56,691 --> 00:17:13,288
Assignment 2, question 2. Holmes removes addressing modes, one at a time. In part a, loads and stores cannot use a register plus an offset. Rewrite load word x1, 16 of x2. Pause the video and try it.

102
00:17:13,738 --> 00:17:26,197
This question has three addressing modes. Displacement: a register plus a constant. Register-indirect: the value in a register. Absolute: a constant in the instruction.

103
00:17:26,647 --> 00:17:39,297
The solution does the add in a separate add immediate. x3 gets x2 plus 16. Then load word x1 with offset 0 from x3. That is register-indirect.

104
00:17:39,747 --> 00:17:44,569
The cost is one more instruction, and one temporary register, x3.

105
00:17:45,019 --> 00:18:02,597
Part b. Now loads and stores have only absolute addressing. The address is a 20-bit constant in the instruction. Translate load word x1, 16 of x2. Hint: change the bits of an instruction. Pause the video and try it.

106
00:18:03,047 --> 00:18:15,485
The problem: x2 plus 16 is known only when the program runs. But an absolute load holds a constant. So the program must build the load instruction while it runs.

107
00:18:15,935 --> 00:18:25,428
The data region has a template instruction, load word x1, 0. The first load copies this word into x3, as data.

108
00:18:25,878 --> 00:18:35,222
Add immediate x3, x3, 16. The immediate field is in the low bits of the word. So this adds 16 to the immediate.

109
00:18:35,672 --> 00:18:49,432
Add x3, x3, x2. Now the immediate field is x2 plus 16. With the example x2 equal to hex 1 0 0 0, it is hex 1 0 1 0.

110
00:18:49,882 --> 00:18:58,437
Store word x3 at the label underscore l w. This overwrites the no operation instruction with the new load.

111
00:18:58,887 --> 00:19:09,063
The P C then reaches underscore l w and runs the new load. This works while x2 plus 16 fits in the 20 immediate bits.

112
00:19:09,513 --> 00:19:24,553
Part c. Now jumps take a 20-bit absolute address, and jump register is not allowed. multiply gets its return address in x31. Rewrite it without jump register. Pause the video and try it.

113
00:19:25,003 --> 00:19:31,168
Use the same method as part b. Build a jump to the return address while the program runs.

114
00:19:31,618 --> 00:19:38,573
The data region has a template, jump to 0. Load word x6 copies this word into x6.

115
00:19:39,023 --> 00:19:53,892
Add x31 to x6. The immediate field now holds the return address. With the example x31 equal to hex 1 0 0 4, the word is jump to hex 1 0 0 4.

116
00:19:54,342 --> 00:20:01,809
Store word x6 at the label return. The no operation instruction there becomes the jump to the caller.

117
00:20:02,259 --> 00:20:08,147
The rest of the code does not change. It sets result and i to 0, and runs the loop.

118
00:20:08,597 --> 00:20:14,143
When the loop ends, the P C reaches return. The new jump goes back to the caller.

119
00:20:14,593 --> 00:20:25,004
Here is the recap. One. Parameters go in a0 to a7. Results come back in a0 and a1. The return address is in R A.

120
00:20:25,454 --> 00:20:32,793
Two. Jump and link puts P C plus 4 in R A, and jumps. Jump register R A jumps back.

121
00:20:33,243 --> 00:20:41,819
Three. The stack grows down. To push n words, subtract 4 n from S P, then store. To pop, load, then add 4 n.

122
00:20:42,269 --> 00:20:53,618
Four. The callee must restore S P and the s registers. The caller must save R A, the a registers and the t registers, if it needs them after a call.

123
00:20:54,068 --> 00:20:59,636
Five. A leaf function that uses only t and a registers needs no stack.

124
00:21:00,086 --> 00:21:10,070
Six. Without an addressing mode, use an extra instruction, or build the instruction word while the program runs. That is the end of video 4.
